# 🏫 Linked List Classroom (Singly Linked List, in C)

---

## 🔹 1. Starting Point: "Sir, Why Linked List at all?"
**Student’s doubt:**  
- “Sir, arrays already exist. Why do I need this linked list headache?”  

**Answer:**  
- Arrays have **fixed size** → If you declare `int arr[10]`, memory is fixed at compile time. What if you need 11 elements?  
- Insertion/Deletion in arrays is costly → To insert at beginning, you shift all elements right. That’s **O(n)** time.  
- Linked list solves both:
  - Dynamic size (grow/shrink at runtime).
  - Insertion/Deletion at beginning/end is **O(1)**.

👉 So, Linked List = **flexible train** vs Array = **rigid bus**.  

---

## 🔹 2. Node Definition

```c
struct Node {
    int data;
    struct Node* next;
};
```

**Why `struct Node* next` instead of `struct Node next`?**  
- Because if it were `struct Node next`, it would create **infinite nesting** (a Node inside Node inside Node...).  
- Pointer saves memory → it just stores an address.  

👉 Trick: Think of `next` like a **Google Maps location pin** pointing to the next bogie.  

---

## 🔹 3. Types of Linked List
- **Singly Linked List** → One direction.
- **Doubly Linked List** → Forward + Backward.
- **Circular Linked List** → Last node points to first.  

👉 In exams, **Singly LL** is always first. Others are extensions.  

---

## 🔹 4. Core Operations (Step-by-Step with WHY/HOW)

---

### (A) Insertion at Beginning

**Process:**
1. Create a node.
2. Make new node point to current head.
3. Update head to new node.

**Code:**
```c
struct Node* insertAtStart(struct Node* head, int value) {
    struct Node* newNode = (struct Node*)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
    newNode->data = value;         // step 1: put value
    newNode->next = head;          // step 2: connect to old head
    head = newNode;                // step 3: update head
    return head;
}
```

**Student asks:**  
- “Why `malloc`?”  
👉 Because linked list nodes are created at runtime (dynamic memory). If you do `struct Node newNode;`, node will vanish when function ends.  

- “Why return head?”  
👉 Because head changes (new node is now first). If you don’t return, you’ll lose new head.  

**Dry Run:**  
- Start: `head → [10|NULL]`  
- Insert 20 → `head → [20|] → [10|NULL]`  

---

### (B) Insertion at End

**Process:**
1. Create new node.
2. Traverse till last node.
3. Link last node to new node.

**Code:**
```c
struct Node* insertAtEnd(struct Node* head, int value) {
    struct Node* newNode = (struct Node*)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
    newNode->data = value;
    newNode->next = NULL;

    if (head == NULL)   // empty list
        return newNode;

    struct Node* temp = head;
    while (temp->next != NULL) {   // go to last
        temp = temp->next;
    }
    temp->next = newNode;
    return head;
}
```

**Student asks:**  
- “Why check if `head == NULL`?”  
👉 Because if the list is empty, new node becomes the first node directly.  
- “Why `newNode->next = NULL`?”  
👉 Because last node should not point anywhere else.  

---

### (C) Deletion at Beginning

**Process:**
1. Move head to next node.
2. Free old head.  

**Code:**
```c
struct Node* deleteAtStart(struct Node* head) {
    if (head == NULL) return NULL;   // empty list
    struct Node* temp = head;
    head = head->next;
    free(temp);                      // free memory
    return head;
}
```

**Student asks:**  
- “Why free?”  
👉 Because memory was allocated using malloc. If not freed → **memory leak**.  
- “What if list is empty?”  
👉 Always check first, else `head->next` crashes program.  

---

### (D) Deletion at End

**Process:**
1. Traverse to second-last node.
2. Free last node.
3. Make second-last’s next = NULL.

**Code:**
```c
struct Node* deleteAtEnd(struct Node* head) {
    if (head == NULL) return NULL;      // empty
    if (head->next == NULL) {           // single node
        free(head);
        return NULL;
    }

    struct Node* temp = head;
    while (temp->next->next != NULL) {  // stop at 2nd last
        temp = temp->next;
    }
    free(temp->next);                   // free last
    temp->next = NULL;
    return head;
}
```

**Student asks:**  
- “Why stop at `temp->next->next != NULL` instead of `temp != NULL`?”  
👉 Because you need pointer to second-last node, not NULL.  

---

### (E) Searching

**Process:** Traverse and compare each node.

**Code:**
```c
int search(struct Node* head, int key) {
    struct Node* temp = head;
    while (temp != NULL) {
        if (temp->data == key) return 1;   // found
        temp = temp->next;
    }
    return 0;  // not found
}
```

**Student asks:**  
- “Why not binary search?”  
👉 Linked List is not stored in contiguous memory, so you can’t do index jumps. Only linear traversal works.  

---

### (F) Traversal (Print All)

```c
void printList(struct Node* head) {
    struct Node* temp = head;
    while (temp != NULL) {
        printf("%d -> ", temp->data);
        temp = temp->next;
    }
    printf("NULL\n");
}
```

**Trick:**  
- Always end with `NULL` → shows end of list in exam diagrams.  

---

### (G) Sorting (Bubble Sort)

```c
void sortList(struct Node* head) {
    struct Node* i, *j;
    int temp;
    for (i = head; i != NULL; i = i->next) {
        for (j = i->next; j != NULL; j = j->next) {
            if (i->data > j->data) {
                temp = i->data;
                i->data = j->data;
                j->data = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

**Student asks:**  
- “Sir, why swap data instead of nodes?”  
👉 Swapping pointers is harder; swapping data is easier (exams expect that).  

---

## 🔹 5. Menu Driven Program (All-in-One Template)

👉 This is your **exam weapon**. If you write this, you cover **all LL questions**.

```c
