# 🏫 What is a Circular Linked List (CLL)?

- In a **Singly Linked List (SLL)**:  
  Last node’s `next = NULL`  

- In a **Circular Linked List (CLL)**:  
  Last node’s `next = head`  

So the list forms a **circle**:  
```
head → [10] → [20] → [30] → back to head
```

---

# 🔹 Node Structure
```c
struct Node {
    int data;
    struct Node* next;
};
```

(Same as SLL — only difference is how we manage links.)

---

# 🔹 Traversal in CLL

Unlike SLL (which ends at `NULL`), here you **loop until you reach `head` again**.

```c
void printCLL(struct Node* head) {
    if (head == NULL) return;

    struct Node* temp = head;
    do {
        printf("%d -> ", temp->data);
        temp = temp->next;
    } while (temp != head);

    printf("(back to head)\n");
}
```

